    "knowledge_point": "Solving for Displacement",
    "problem": "A ball is dropped from a height and hits the ground in $3$ seconds. Find the distance the ball covered during its fall. \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*",
    "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the ball is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The ball falls for $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(0)(3)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(3)^{2} \\\\  & =0+4.9(9) \\\\  & =44.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $44.1\\,\\text{m}.$",
